 ## Changelog
+- 2026-10-14: C2ME idea 2: the light engine does a block update's work with far fewer lookups (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +1 test `light::tests::relit_matches_the_plain_formula`, not run; `light_settles_after_edits` is the settled-result check and must still pass). Chosen as the best lever for block-update speed: every edit (`set_quiet` -> `light_after_set`, flowing water, TNT, tree growth) queues light regions, and `mesh_pending` only queues a mesh job while `light_queue` is empty, so the light engine's throughput is how fast a block update becomes visible. Source: the allocation / lookup trimming side of C2ME (`c2me-opts-allocs`); the threaded light engine (`c2me-threading-lighting`) is NOT done: it needs mutable access to the chunk light arrays across borders, a rewrite of `update_region` / `init_chunk` that is too risky to write blind.
+    - Cost found by reading `world/chunks/light.rs`: a cell of `update_region` read its own light, block, open-sky test and six neighbours with ~9 `HashMap` lookups; each CHANGED cell added `set_saved_light` (`chunk_exists` + `put_light` + `mark_dirty`, which built a `Vec` per call: a heap allocation) and 3-6 `neighbor_changed` (`chunk_exists` + `sees_sky` / `raw_block` + `saved_light` + `schedule_light`'s own `chunk_exists`): ~30-40 lookups and one allocation per changed cell. `run_light` also read `Instant::now()` after EVERY region, most of which are one cell.
+    - Now: `relit` reads a cell from its chunk entry once (the neighbours inside the same chunk straight from its arrays, the ones across a border through `saved_light` as before); `neighbor_changed` is one lookup; `set_saved_light` has no extra `chunk_exists`; `mark_dirty` fills a 3-key array instead of a `Vec`; `run_light` reads the clock after a region of more than 256 cells or every 32nd region. Nothing else changed: the light values, their order of evaluation and the queue are the same, so the settled light is the same (`relit_matches_the_plain_formula` compares it with the old formula on borders, corners and the world's top and bottom).
+    - ponytail: the 4 ms `LIGHT_BUDGET` can now overshoot by up to 31 one-cell regions (microseconds). Not touched: the random-tick scan (`random_ticks`, ~150 chunks x 80 draws, tens of microseconds a tick), `ticks::step` and `notify`. Still open from the C2ME audit: save writes on an I/O thread, populate + light on the workers, bit-exact noise speedups, the threaded light engine. UNVERIFIED on a device: the actual gain (the per-cell cost should drop several-fold, but nothing was measured).
 - 2026-10-14: sneak (b1.7.3) + PE d-pad with 8 directions (written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run; +3 tests `physics::tests::sneaking_stops_at_the_edge_and_holds_on_a_ladder`, `touch_ui::tests::{dpad_arrow_shows_its_two_corners_and_walks_diagonally, sneak_button_toggles_and_survives_pause, second_dpad_finger_is_ignored}`, `hud::tests::gui_sheet_has_the_sprites` extended; none run; the generator and the sprite sheet were run and looked at).
     - Sneak, ported from `MovementInputFromOptions`, `Entity.moveEntity`, `EntityPlayerSP.onLivingUpdate`, `EntityLiving.moveEntityWithHeading`: both input axes x0.3 (`lib.rs`, next to the move vector); `physics::sneak` (one shared function, called before `step`): a grounded sneaking box cannot walk off an edge (the step is cut in 0.05 slices until a collision box is within a block below, x then z, as the Java loop does), and a sneaking player on a ladder stops falling; the eye hangs 0.08 low (`ySize` 0.2 set each tick, x0.4 by `moveEntity`: `App.sneak_y`, decays x0.4 a tick when released). The hitbox does not change (b1.7.3 has no crouch box). Not done: the sneaking player model / name tag (no player model), sneak-dismount (b1.7.3 has none in the source read, jump still gets off a pig).
     - Touch: the floating analog stick is gone (`stick_vector`, `PointerRole::Move`). `LayoutRects.dpad` = 8 cells in `DIRS` order (up, down, left, right, up_left, up_right, down_left, down_right) tiled without gaps (a button is drawn `dpad_gap` smaller), the centre cell is sneak (a toggle, `TouchUi.sneak`, kept over pause), `jump` is a rect now. Holding an arrow shows its two corner buttons (up: up-left + up-right, left: up-left + down-left, ...); sliding onto a corner walks that diagonal (`DIRS` normalised, like `moveFlying`), sliding to another arrow swaps the corners, lifting hides them. Look is every press that is no button (it was the right half).
